# harness-adapters

Load this before any harness-specific judgement about a Crew's pane.
It is a reference, not a procedure: read the rows for the harness the Crew actually runs on and go back to what you were doing.

Which harness a Crew runs on is printed by `{{.MatevBin}} crew list {{.ProjectName}}` in the HARNESS column, and by `mate crew spawn` in its first line.
Never guess it from what you yourself run on; you run on `{{.Harness}}` and a Crew defaults to Codex.

## What you can and cannot do to a pane

You have exactly three pane operations, and none of them sends a raw key:

- `mate peek <project> <crew>` reads the last lines of the pane and changes nothing.
- `mate send <project> <crew> "<one line>"` types one line and presses enter until the composer clears.
- `mate crew stop <project> <crew>` ends the agent and closes the pane.

There is no interrupt key, no escape, no arrow key, and no bare enter.
So "interrupt the Crew" in practice means one of: send a line the harness will accept mid-turn with `--queue`, or stop the Crew and spawn a fresh one with the same brief plus a note of what was already tried.
Anything that would need a keystroke is an escalation to the captain, not something you improvise.

## Composer states you will be told about

`mate send` and `mate state` classify the pane before acting, and report what they saw:

| Reading | Screen | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| busy / `working · source: pane` | the harness's mid-turn indicator | A turn is running. A send refuses unless you pass `--queue`. |
| empty | an idle composer | Safe to send. |
| pending | text sitting unsubmitted in the composer | Somebody else is typing there. Never type over it. |
| unknown | anything else | A dialog, a scrolled transcript, or a harness that has not finished starting. Peek and read it. |

An `unknown` reading is the one that needs this skill.
Peek, match what you see against the sections below, and act on that - do not re-send and do not stop the Crew until you know which of the two it is.

## codex

Codex is the default Crew harness.

| Fact | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Busy signature | `esc to interrupt`, usually inside a `• Working (Xs • esc to interrupt)` footer |
| Idle composer | a bordered input box with the cursor in it |
| Directory trust | `Do you trust the contents of this directory?`, asked per absolute path |
| Instructions | the brief is delivered as a file in the worktree that Codex reads at startup |

Codex confirms trust for each absolute path separately, and a Crew worktree is always a path it has never seen, so the dialog appears on essentially every spawn.
`mate crew spawn` answers it during launch and prints a `note: answered the codex directory-trust dialog for <worktree>` line on stderr when it did.
If you peek and the dialog is still on screen, the spawn did not complete: report that to the captain rather than sending a line at it, because a line typed into that dialog is not an answer to it.

A first prompt that Codex never picked up leaves the pane idle with the brief line visible in the composer.
That is a `pending` reading, and the fix is not to type: peek, then report it, because the Crew has not started and its worktree is empty.

## claude

Claude Code is what you run on, and what a Crew runs on when a spawn is given `--harness claude`.

| Fact | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Busy signature | `esc to interrupt` |
| Idle composer | a `>` prompt box |
| Directory trust | a trust confirmation whose default highlight is the refusing option |
| Instructions | the brief is delivered as a launch-time system prompt, so nothing lands in the worktree |

The trust dialog's default highlight is the choice that exits the agent.
This is exactly why you have no key-sending command: a blind confirmation here kills the Crew rather than starting it.
Claude also shows a one-time bypass-permissions acceptance screen the first time a given configuration directory sees it; it looks like a dialog and reads as `unknown` the same way.

Claude renders a greyed-out suggestion inside an otherwise-empty composer after a turn ends.
It is not typed text and the classifier ignores it, but it is why a peek of an idle Claude pane can look like it has something in the box when `state` says the composer is empty; believe `state`.

## Adding a harness

Only Codex and Claude Code are verified for a mate Crew.
If the captain asks for another, say that it is not verified and that a Crew on it would be unsupervisable, because every row above - the busy signature especially - has to be measured before `mate state` can tell working from wedged.
